# T9AMAIL — Project Handover

## Goal
Enable email read/reply functionality on an HMD feature phone (2.4" screen)
via Opera Mini Extreme. Opera Extreme strips pages to plain text/basic HTML —
no JS, minimal CSS, single-column only. All mail processing happens
server-side (VPS); the phone only ever fetches simple rendered HTML pages.

## Architecture principle
Avoid live IMAP calls at the web-serving stage entirely. `mbsync` handles
sync (cron'd), and everything downstream operates on local files only —
fast, inspectable, no protocol round-trips while a user is waiting on a
page load.

## Two-module split (deliberate, not accidental)
- **View Module** — read-only. Pages the mailbox, lets the user browse,
  displays a selected message. No SMTP awareness.
- **Compose Module** — construct + send a reply. Not yet built. Only
  shares `mail.raw` as input with the View Module; no shared code.
  (Originally split into "Reply" + "SMTP" but merged into one "Compose"
  module since they're one continuous workflow with no natural seam.)

`mail.raw` (a single copied raw `.eml`-equivalent message) is the sole
bridge between the two modules — deliberately duplicating small bits of
field-extraction logic on the Compose side rather than sharing code with
View, since the two have different correctness requirements.

## Working chain (View Module) — proven end to end
```
mbsync posteo          → syncs Posteo IMAP into ~/Mail/INBOX/{new,cur}
merger.sh               → flattens new/+cur into one merged dir (fixes a
                           real bug: messages sit in new/ until an MUA
                           touches them, moving them to cur/ — any script
                           scanning cur/ only will miss recent mail)
pager.sh                → scans mailbox, writes headers.txt (UID, read
                           status, From, Subject, Date, Warning flag)
browse.sh                → interactive one-at-a-time viewer/menu:
                           [n]ext [p]revious [v]iew [r]eply [q]uit
                           - view: decodes + displays body, writes mail.raw
                           - reply: writes mail.raw, hands off (stub only)
choose.sh                → older list-style alternative to browse.sh
                           (still present, not necessarily needed going
                           forward — browse.sh is the current preferred UI)
tmail.sh                → wrapper: mbsync → merger → pager → browse/choose
```

## Shared helper library
`mail_helpers.sh` — sourced (not executed) by pager.sh / choose.sh /
browse.sh / rawmail2md.sh. Contains:
- `clean_from` — strips `<email>` brackets, prefers display name, falls
  back to bare address if no display name exists
- `decode_subject` — RFC 2047 decode (`=?utf-8?Q?...?=` etc.), via python3
- `trim_date` — reformats RFC 5322 dates for display. **Uses Python's
  `email.utils.parsedate_to_datetime`, NOT the `date -d` shell command** —
  `date -d` silently converts to the container's local timezone, which
  corrupted timestamps (shifted messages to the wrong day). This was a
  real bug, found and fixed — don't revert to `date -d`.

Rationale for a shared library: within the View module, field-cleaning
rules (from/subject/date) are genuinely shared logic, not incidental
duplication — a fix in one place should apply everywhere. This does NOT
extend across the View/Compose boundary by design.

## Filter 1 vs Filter 2 (content-safety/warning model)
- **Filter 1** (in `pager.sh`) — cheap, advisory, per-message, runs across
  the whole mailbox every time. Just checks `Content-Type:` header; flags
  anything that isn't plain `text/plain` (or missing the header entirely)
  with `Warning: !` in headers.txt. Not mission-critical — worst case is
  a message displays messily, user just moves on. Deliberately broad
  rather than trying to precisely classify HTML vs multipart vs
  attachments at this stage.
- **Filter 2** (in `rawmail2md.sh` / `browse.sh`'s `render_body()`) —
  heavier processing on the single selected `mail.raw`. Handles:
  - quoted-printable decoding (via python3 `quopri` module — real test
    mail from Kiwibank required this; raw quoted-printable has soft
    line-break artifacts like `Suffi=`/`x:` splitting words)
  - HTML tag stripping (crude `sed`, works for simple tags; would leak
    `<style>`/`<script>` contents if encountered — not yet an issue with
    test mail so far)
  - Does NOT yet handle: base64-encoded bodies, or genuine
    `multipart/alternative` messages with MIME boundaries (mutt shows
    these are common in real mail — a `text/plain` part alongside
    `text/html`; correct behavior would be to prefer the plain part).
    **This is a known, real gap, not yet built.**

## View → HTML rendering plan (also not yet built, but decided)
```
mail.raw → rawmail2md.sh → body.md → `markdown` (the CLI tool,
           /usr/bin/markdown, already used elsewhere by the user) → body.html
```
Chosen over hand-wrapping HTML in bash because:
- `markdown` handles HTML-escaping correctly and automatically (avoids
  a real bug that was found: unescaped `<`/`>`/`&` in a raw body would
  break when wrapped directly in `<pre>` tags)
- Matches the user's existing authoring workflow (writes Markdown for a
  Jekyll site; bash scripts are being treated the same way — as
  inspectable "sketches" that a mechanical tool converts, not something
  requiring deep HTML knowledge)

One bug already found and fixed in this path: `markdown` auto-links
anything in `<email@domain>` form (RFC autolink syntax) — the `From:`
field must have angle brackets stripped (via `clean_from`) *before*
being written to the `.md` file, or it renders as an obfuscated mailto
link instead of plain text.

## Compose Module — not started (~0% done)
Planned shape, not yet built:
```
mail.raw → extract From/Subject/Message-ID →
  build RFC 5322 headers (To ← From, Subject: Re: ..., In-Reply-To,
  References ← Message-ID) → prompt/accept body text →
  pipe to `msmtp -t < reply.rfc`
```
Minimum required RFC 5322 fields confirmed: `To`, blank line, body.
`Subject` almost always wanted. `In-Reply-To`/`References` needed for
proper threading in the recipient's client. `From`/`Date`/`Message-ID`
can be omitted — msmtp/server fill sensible defaults.

`msmtp` is installed and configured (`~/.msmtprc`, account `posteo`,
port 587 STARTTLS, `chmod 600`). Sending has been smoke-tested manually
and confirmed working.

## Deployment status
- VPS: domain is **omegasemantic.org**. Plan is to serve a minimal
  static "hello world" first (proves domain/TLS/Opera Mini reachability
  independent of the mail pipeline), then move `mbsync` + all View
  Module scripts onto the VPS itself (cron'd, not laptop-dependent).
- Local dev: `localhost:PORT` via Bottle — good for logic/plumbing, but
  **cannot** be used to test real Opera Mini Extreme rendering, since
  Opera's proxy needs to fetch from the public internet, not localhost.
  Periodic real-device checks against the VPS (not just at the end)
  were agreed as the right cadence, to catch Opera-specific rendering
  quirks early.
- Bottle chosen as the web framework (lighter than Flask). Philosophy
  agreed: **bash scripts stay as bash** ("sketch in bash, port to Python
  later if someone wants to") — Bottle routes are meant to be thin
  dispatchers, likely calling existing bash scripts via `subprocess`,
  not a rewrite of the logic into Python. This is a deliberate choice,
  not a placeholder.
- Bottle installed on VPS; a minimal `hello.html` static-file-serving
  test app was scaffolded (steps given, not yet confirmed working by
  the user — this is the last concrete action before the thread ended).

