## Immediate next steps (in likely priority order)
1. Confirm the Bottle "hello world" test is actually reachable from
   omegasemantic.org, then test on the real feature phone via Opera
   Mini Extreme (not just a desktop browser).
2. Build the Compose Module (reply construction + msmtp send), as its
   own largely independent piece of work, using `mail.raw` as the only
   input contract.
3. Handle genuine `multipart/alternative` MIME messages properly in
   Filter 2 (currently a known gap — common in real mail per mutt's own
   parsing, not yet replicated in the bash scripts).
4. Wire the View Module's bash scripts into Bottle routes (likely via
   `subprocess`, per the stated bash-first philosophy), replacing the
   CLI menu interaction with HTTP routes/links — conceptually
   `read -p` prompts become form/link submissions carrying UID as a
   route parameter; note state (like the "current UID" browse.sh keeps
   in a variable) needs to travel via the URL, since HTTP requests are
   stateless and nothing persists between clicks the way it does in a
   single running bash script.
5. Eventually move `mbsync` + cron off the laptop and onto the VPS, so
   sync doesn't depend on the laptop being awake/online.

## User context (do not need to re-derive, but don't over-reference)
Comfortable with vim, bash, ChromeOS/Crostini (Linux container), has an
existing Jekyll site on GitHub and already uses `/usr/bin/markdown` for
lightweight tasks — this is why Markdown was chosen as the View
Module's intermediate rendering format rather than hand-rolled HTML.
Prefers working incrementally with real inspectable files at each
stage over black-box tooling; explicitly values file-based checkpoints
over in-memory/streaming approaches. Not deeply familiar with email
protocol internals (UID semantics, MIME, IMAP) but has been picking it
up quickly through this build.
